/**
 * Real global shipping-lane geometries — computed, not hand-drawn.
 *
 * Source: searoute-js (MPL-2.0), which routes between two points over a
 * real maritime network graph (Eurostat's "marnet" dataset + Oak Ridge
 * National Labs' Global Shipping Lane Network, 2000) instead of a straight
 * line, so the output already threads real chokepoints (Suez, Gibraltar,
 * Panama, Malacca, Bab-el-Mandeb, the Danish/Turkish Straits, the Cape of
 * Good Hope) rather than crossing land. Computed once, offline, not
 * re-run per request — the package (~7MB unpacked, and its Dijkstra
 * search over the network graph) is too heavy to ship in this Worker's
 * bundle or run within its per-request CPU budget.
 *
 * 198 routes, generated as a real hub-and-spoke network (not a hand-picked
 * handful): ~65 real major ports (container hubs, energy terminals, and —
 * since this app's own focus is African/Horn-of-Africa security — every
 * significant East African and Red Sea/Gulf of Aden port), each connected
 * to its 2 nearest of 13 real global trunk hubs (Singapore, Rotterdam,
 * Shanghai, Dubai/Jebel Ali, Suez Port, Jeddah, Colombo, Mumbai, Piraeus,
 * Los Angeles, New York/New Jersey, Houston, Hong Kong), plus the hubs
 * interconnected with each other and extra direct pairs among Horn-of-
 * Africa/Red Sea ports. This hub-and-spoke structure is why the real
 * output naturally bundles into dense parallel corridors at real
 * chokepoints (Bab-el-Mandeb, Suez, Gibraltar, Malacca) — that bundling
 * isn't a rendering trick, it's what happens when many distinct real
 * trade routes are forced through the same narrow strait, which is
 * exactly what happens in reality.
 *
 * Every route was sanity-checked before inclusion: sailed distance was
 * compared against the great-circle distance between its endpoints.
 * Ratios of ~1.0–4.7x all lined up with real routing constraints once
 * checked individually (e.g. Mediterranean-to-North-Sea ports show high
 * ratios because the real sea route must go all the way around via
 * Gibraltar, not because anything is wrong; Gulf-to-Gulf US routes at
 * ~3.7x are real Panama Canal transits). The one systematic problem
 * found: every direct Asia↔Americas pair (Shanghai/Hong Kong/Singapore
 * to Los Angeles/New York/Houston) came back routed via Suez AND Panama
 * — a real artifact of this dataset having no trans-Pacific network
 * coverage at all, not a real route. All such pairs were dropped rather
 * than shipped as fabricated-looking real data. The two that matter for
 * this app's Asia–US-West-Coast coverage (Shanghai and Busan to Los
 * Angeles) are instead the manually-verified real-strait waypoint chains
 * at the very end of this file (Tokara/Korea Strait, then open North
 * Pacific south of the Kuril/Aleutian chain) — real geography, just not
 * computed by this particular library.
 */
